  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSkies are mostly clear this morning under weak offshore flow.\nSurface pressure gradients to the Great Basin have weakened\nsignificantly over the past 24 hr, though not much change has\noccurred to the Mojave Desert. Northeast to east winds are gusting\n20-30 mph, mainly in the Cajon Pass and along the coastal slopes\nof the San Diego County mountains and Santa Ana Mountains. Winds\nare forecast to strengthen slightly through the morning, then\nweaken again this afternoon. Otherwise dry and warm today with\nhighs 7-15 degrees above normal west of the mountains and around\n3-5 degrees above normal elsewhere. High temperatures for inland\ncoastal areas and the western valleys could reach 90 degrees in\nspots. \n\nOffshore flow continues to weaken on Monday, then remains weak\nthrough at least Thursday. Weak upper level ridging remains in\nplace through Tuesday, then the ridge amplifies across the\nSouthwest on Wednesday ahead of a trough developing off the coast,\nboth of which shift east on Thursday. This will maintain warm and\ndry weather for much of the week with widespread high temperatures\n5-10 degrees above normal: mid to upper 80s for the coastal areas\nand valleys, low 60s to low 70s in the mountains, mid to upper 70s\nin the high desert, and mid to upper 80s in the low desert.\n\nGlobal ensembles have trended slower with the onset of\nprecipitation late in the week, with slight chances now beginning\nlate Friday. Significant differences remain in the evolution of\nthe upper level pattern as the jet tries to split. Around 47% of\nthe ensemble members (as well as the deterministic EC and GFS) for\na deep closed low in the southern stream and digs it south off the\ncoast of CA and potentially into northern Mexico late Friday into\nSaturday. Models are notoriously poor at forecasting the\ntrack/timing of such systems, so if this pattern holds the\nprecipitation forecast will likely change - potentially\nsignificantly - over the next few days. Around 31% of the members\nshow a weaker open wave developing in the southern stream, which \nwould be more progressive (precipitation starting and ending \nearlier with less accumulation). The remaining 22% does not \nproduce any split flow with zonal winds across So Cal Friday and \nSaturday. Current NBM forecast favors the further south closed low\nscenario with higher precipitation probabilities over San Diego\nCounty on Saturday (20-30%), decreasing to the north (15-20%). NBM\nchances of moderate precipitation (48 hr total of at least 0.50\")\nremains around 20% for the mountains westward. The only certain \nthing for Friday and Saturday is cooling with increasing cloud \ncover.",
